  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...One more night of frost potential will give way\nto a warming trend early this week. \n\nHigh pressure remains centered along the Mid Atlantic coast this\nafternoon but will drift southwestward tonight. Low-level flow \nwill turn westerly allowing for less of a marine influence. At\nthe same time, temperatures aloft will begin rising. The surface\ngradient is weak enough that forecast soundings indicate sharp\ndecoupling. This will send lows into the 30s again, particularly\nin outlying and low lying areas. A Frost Advisory has been\nissued for the piedmont where the growing season remains active,\nas these areas will likely decouple best. The advisory is based\non the forecast lows, although there is some potential with dew\npoints starting in the 40s and the moderating airmass, we may\nultimately end up with just a heavy dew even if temperatures\ndrop to the mid 30s. Ridgetops meanwhile will stay in the 40s.\nExpect river valley fog to develop again, although some guidance\nsuggests the fog may affect more areas than in previous nights.\n\nExpect temperatures to rise above normal (upper 60s to lower \n70s) by Monday afternoon as the warming airmass mixes to the \nsurface. Minimal cloud cover and light winds will remain \npersistent.\n\nA backdoor front will drop into the area Tuesday, trailing low\npressure moving into the Canadian Maritimes. Forcing and\nmoisture with this front are limited, with the main effects\nbeing a few extra clouds and a wind shift to the north. Minimal\nlow level cold advection will mean temperatures likely remain\nabove normal for Tuesday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions persist with increasing\ntemperatures through the end of the week.\n\nUpper-level ridging builds over the Mid-Atlantic through the end of \nthe week, resulting in continued high pressure and dry, stable \nconditions across the region. After a slight cool down Wednesday\nbehind the backdoor front, temperatures should gradually warm \nsome, with afternoon highs reaching the mid-70s by Friday with \novernight lows in the 50s. As the front stalls and starts \nlifting back north, some extra clouds may form in the warm \nadvection pattern Wednesday into early Thursday. Mostly sunny\nskies will return Thursday into Friday.\n\nA few models point to this upper-level ridging breaking down \ninto zonal flow overhead by the start of the weekend, although \nsurface high pressure could linger for a bit longer. Overall, \nthere is still no significant precipitation in the forecast \nthrough the end of the week, although will continue to monitor a\npotential cold front off to the west that could approach the \nregion by the end of next weekend.",
